                "2": {
                    "name": "text_block",
                    "id": 311,
                    "position_x": 917,
                    "position_y": 45,
                    "width": 768,
                    "height": 876,
                    "visibility": "show_all",
                    "draggable": true,
                    "presentation_state": "show_all",
                    "properties": {
                        "0": {
                            "name": "block",
                            "value": "Cuts an array into a number of pieces along one axis, and sends each piece from its own outlet.\n\nUse it to separate an array that holds several things side by side - the left and right halves of an image, the x, y and z columns of a list of points, the first and second half of a recording.\n\nTHE NUMBER OF PIECES IS FIXED:\nIt is the first argument, and it decides how many outlets the node has (array 0, array 1, ...), so it cannot change on a live node - retype the node to change it. The smallest is 2. If the axis does not divide evenly, the first pieces get one extra: 7 rows in 3 pieces are 3, 2 and 2.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.split <pieces: int> <axis: int>\nDefaults: 2 pieces, axis 0.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.split 3\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list is turned into one). Every array that arrives is cut and the pieces sent, array 0 first. Only this inlet makes the node send.\n\naxis (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich axis to cut across: 0 cuts the rows into groups (horizontal cuts), 1 cuts the columns into groups (vertical cuts). Negative counts from the end. An axis the array does not have - or one shorter than the number of pieces - sends nothing. Changing it does NOT send by itself; in the demo a metro inside make_F re-sends the F ten times a second.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\narray 0, array 1, ...:\nThe pieces, in order. Each has the same number of dimensions as the input; only the length along the axis is smaller.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nnp.split 3 cuts the 7 by 6 F into three bands of rows: the top 3 rows (the bar), then 2 and 2. The lists show each piece's shape. Type 1 into axis and it cuts the columns instead, three slices 2 columns wide: the stem, then the two halves of the arms.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.concatenate puts pieces back together. np.stack joins along a new axis. np.reshape refolds without cutting. t.chunk and t.tensor_split, on the t.cat page, are the PyTorch versions.",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        },
                        "1": {
                            "name": "lock",
                            "value": true,
                            "value_type": "bool"
                        },
                        "2": {
                            "name": "width",
                            "value": 760,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "3": {
                            "name": "height",
                            "value": 860,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "4": {
                            "name": "text_size",
                            "value": "30",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        }
                    },
                    "text": "Cuts an array into a number of pieces along one axis, and sends each piece from its own outlet.\n\nUse it to separate an array that holds several things side by side - the left and right halves of an image, the x, y and z columns of a list of points, the first and second half of a recording.\n\nTHE NUMBER OF PIECES IS FIXED:\nIt is the first argument, and it decides how many outlets the node has (array 0, array 1, ...), so it cannot change on a live node - retype the node to change it. The smallest is 2. If the axis does not divide evenly, the first pieces get one extra: 7 rows in 3 pieces are 3, 2 and 2.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.split <pieces: int> <axis: int>\nDefaults: 2 pieces, axis 0.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.split 3\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list is turned into one). Every array that arrives is cut and the pieces sent, array 0 first. Only this inlet makes the node send.\n\naxis (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich axis to cut across: 0 cuts the rows into groups (horizontal cuts), 1 cuts the columns into groups (vertical cuts). Negative counts from the end. An axis the array does not have - or one shorter than the number of pieces - sends nothing. Changing it does NOT send by itself; in the demo a metro inside make_F re-sends the F ten times a second.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\narray 0, array 1, ...:\nThe pieces, in order. Each has the same number of dimensions as the input; only the length along the axis is smaller.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nnp.split 3 cuts the 7 by 6 F into three bands of rows: the top 3 rows (the bar), then 2 and 2. The lists show each piece's shape. Type 1 into axis and it cuts the columns instead, three slices 2 columns wide: the stem, then the two halves of the arms.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.concatenate puts pieces back together. np.stack joins along a new axis. np.reshape refolds without cutting. t.chunk and t.tensor_split, on the t.cat page, are the PyTorch versions."
                },
